proposals.progress:
**Three readouts, at three grains, all permanent.**

**Overall, as a strip.** A 4px full-bleed bar appended as the last child of the existing `.chrome` block in `main.ts`, so it inherits the sticky positioning already there and adds no new sticky tier:

```html
<div class="progress-strip" role="progressbar" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="176"
     aria-valuenow="34" aria-valuetext="34 of 176 questions answered"><i style="width:19.3%"></i></div>
```
```css
.progress-strip { height: 4px; background: var(--surface-2); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
.progress-strip i { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--accent);
  transition: width .35s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1); }
.progress-strip.all-done i { background: var(--good); }
```
Four pixels is not a readout on its own, but it is visible on every mode and at every width, and it is the universal shape of "how far through am I".

**Overall, as a figure.** The `.rail-meter` at the top of the sticky rail: `34` in 1.6rem mono tabular numerals, "of 176" beside it, an 8px track, and the word "answered" under it. This is the one she asked for, and being at the top of a sticky rail it is always on screen without eating a row of height.

**Per domain.** The existing `.step-bar` in each of the five stepper tabs, unchanged in markup, now permanently visible because the stepper is sticky. Plus `.step.complete`, already implemented.

**Per section.** The rail's `14/14` counts and glyphs, plus `6 of 9 answered` in the sticky section summary. Today's format is `6/9`; spelling it out reads better and no test depends on that string.

**What makes a question answered.** A score, or Not applicable. Nothing else. `scoring.ts` already computes exactly this: `answered` counts questions with a numeric score, and n/a leaves the denominator, so a file with one n/a reads "34 of 175". Justification and evidence are **not required** and do not affect the count. Three reasons, and I would give her all three: the rubric carries no required flag on any of 176 questions and none of them has `help` or `evidencePrompt` either, so requiredness is not in the data; making prose mandatory on 176 questions guarantees either abandonment or 176 sentences of filler, which is worse for the assessor than silence; and government forms mark a handful of fields mandatory, never all of them. The one hard gate in this app stays the marking gate, which is Dan's rule and already blocks saving.

**Quality without blocking.** `.q-hint` renders when the answer is thin, using the exact predicates already in `flags.ts` so the submit side and the results page can never disagree. At `raw >= 8 && evidence.length === 0`: "An assessor will ask what this 8 is based on." At `raw === 10 && justification.trim().length < 40`: "A 10 with barely a sentence behind it will get challenged." Styled as `.q-hint { color: var(--warn); border-left: 2px solid; padding-left: .5rem; margin-top: .45rem }`, matching the existing `.band`/`.flag` idiom. It does not change the count, does not colour the status edge, and does not block saving.

**Changing a score to a different number.** Silent. No confirmation, no history, no re-animation, no toast. The status edge transitions its colour over 250ms via `transition: background-color .25s ease` and the `.chosen` sentence updates; the counters do not move because the question was already counted. During a first pass people revise constantly, and celebrating or confirming a revision is noise. Deselecting a score, which the current toggle already supports, drops the state back to unanswered, decrements every counter, and shrinks the bars with the same 350ms width transition. No un-celebration, no warning. There is deliberately no revision log on the submit side; the reviewer side already computes deltas against the submitter's number, and the submitter's own second thoughts are not a record anyone needs.

**Animation. Four, all short, all one-shot.**

```css
/* 1. a question becomes answered: 220ms total */
@keyframes q-edge { from { transform: scaleY(0) } to { transform: scaleY(1) } }
@keyframes q-tick { 0% { opacity:0; transform: scale(.55) }
                    60% { opacity:1; transform: scale(1.12) }
                    100% { opacity:1; transform: scale(1) } }
.question.just-answered::before { animation: q-edge 220ms cubic-bezier(.2,.7,.2,1) 1; }
.question.just-answered .q-mark { animation: q-tick 200ms cubic-bezier(.34,1.56,.64,1) 1; }

/* 2. a section becomes complete: one sheen, 520ms */
@keyframes sec-sheen { from { background-position: -140% 0 } to { background-position: 240% 0 } }
.section-summary.just-complete {
  background-image: linear-gradient(115deg, transparent 42%,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 24%, transparent) 50%, transparent 58%);
  background-size: 220% 100%; background-repeat: no-repeat;
  animation: sec-sheen 520ms ease-out 1; }

/* 3. every meter */
.progress-strip i, .rail-meter .meter i, .step-bar i { transition: width .35s cubic-bezier(.4,0,.2,1); }

/* 4. all 176 answered: two pulses, then still */
@keyframes done-glow { 0%,100% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 0 transparent }
                       50% { box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 38%, transparent) } }
.rail-meter.all-done { animation: done-glow 1.4s ease-in-out 2; }
```

What animates, precisely. On the unanswered-to-answered transition of one question: the 4px left edge draws downward from the top over 220ms, and the gutter glyph swaps from nothing to `✓`, arriving over 200ms with a single 12% overshoot. That overshoot is the "sparkly", and it costs nothing in layout because the gutter is a fixed 2.9rem and the glyph occupies the same box as the number above it. Nothing reflows. When a section's last question lands, one 520ms diagonal green sheen crosses the section header and its count chip becomes a `Complete` badge; the section does not collapse. Every meter animates width only, 350ms, on every change. When the last of 176 lands, the rail meter turns `--good` and pulses twice over 2.8s, then stops.

Explicitly not confetti and not particles. This is a Protected B instrument on a government laptop and a burst of confetti over a classification banner would read as a joke about the marking. A single sheen is the installer-progress-bar convention, which is what "sparkly" means in a work tool.

Firing, so an animation plays once and never on load:

```ts
if (wasUnanswered && nowAnswered) {
  wrap.classList.remove('just-answered');
  void wrap.offsetWidth;                       // restart the animation
  wrap.classList.add('just-answered');
  wrap.addEventListener('animationend',
    () => wrap.classList.remove('just-answered'), { once: true });
}
```

`prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` sets `animation: none` on all four and `transition: none` on the meters, and adds `.question.just-answered::before { transform: scaleY(1) }` so the edge is still drawn. Every state that an animation conveys is also conveyed statically: the tick glyph, the edge colour, the `Complete` badge, the `14/14` count, the `.chosen` sentence, the meter width, and the `aria-valuetext`. This matches the existing house pattern, `marking-pulse` with its reduced-motion opt-out.

**The engineering change that makes all of this possible.** Today every score click calls `repaintApp()`, which tears down and rebuilds 4,049 nodes including 67 `<details>`. Under that model a CSS animation replays on every click, `<details>` open state cannot be user-controlled, and focus is destroyed on every score. Split the callback threaded into `questionBlock` and `evidenceEditor`:

```ts
interface Changed { question(): void; rollup(): void; gate(): void; }
```

- `question()` is local: sets `data-state`, the `--edge` custom property, the gutter glyph, `aria-checked` on the eleven buttons, the `.chosen` line, the `.q-hint`, and fires `just-answered`. The existing `paintScores` / `paintChosen` / `paintOther` closures already establish this pattern.
- `rollup()` recomputes `score(rubric, a)` once and writes, through held references and no teardown: the `.progress-strip i` width and its three aria attributes, the `.rail-meter`, the rail's 4 to 6 status items, the open section's summary pill and count, the five stepper bars and counts, and the sticky footer's count sentence. It also debounces a 600ms write into `.sr-live`: "34 of 176 answered", or on section completion "Part 3, Demonstrated Engagement, complete."
- `gate()` re-renders the footer's `.gate` strip and asks `main.ts` for a banner-only redraw, exported as `setPaintChrome(fn)` alongside the existing `setRepaint`.
- The full `repaintApp()` survives for exactly two triggers, both rare and both genuinely global: a change to `initiative.classification`, and a change to `lifecycleStage`, which moves the weights everywhere.

Side effects, all wanted: focus survives a score click, which today it does not, so a screen reader user stops being thrown back to the top of the tree eleven times per question; typing in a justification already avoids repaint by design and now matches everything else; and the 4,049-node rebuild per click disappears, which on a locked-down laptop is the difference between a snappy form and a laggy one.

proposals.risks:
**Print.** This is the sharpest risk, because the current print stylesheet loses both the score selector and every section title, and it will get worse before it gets better. Fixes, all in the one `@media print` block:

```css
@media print {
  .rail, .stepper, .progress-strip, .sticky-footer, .sitefoot, .topbar,
  .ladder-box, .score-row, .na, .sec-next, .q-hint, button, .filelabel { display: none !important; }
  .form-layout { display: block; }
  details { display: block; }
  details > summary { display: block; }          /* was: none - this is why titles vanished */
  .marking-banner.print-only { display: block; } /* was never overridden, so it never printed */
  .card { border: 0; box-shadow: none; padding: 0 0 .7rem; margin: 0 0 .7rem; }
  .question { break-inside: avoid; border: 0; box-shadow: none;
    border-top: 1px solid #999; border-radius: 0; }
  .section-summary { break-after: avoid; position: static; }
  .answer, .evidence { background: none; border: 0; padding: 0; }
}
```

Note what changed and why. `details > summary { display: block }` is the one-line fix for missing section titles, and dropping `.ladder-box` whole is what stops it also printing eleven rungs per question, which was the reason the summary was hidden in the first place. `page-break-inside: avoid` comes off `.card`, where a 4,400px section card can never honour it, and goes onto `.question`, which is now a card-sized unit that can. The score prints because `paintChosen` already writes `7 - Scalable & Secure.` into `.chosen`; the gap is that an unanswered question prints nothing where its answer belongs, so `paintChosen` should write `Not scored` in `.dim` when the score is null, which reads correctly on screen too. The accordion is safe in print because `details { display: block }` opens all of them.

What still will not work: running headers and footers on every printed page. `@page` margin boxes are barely supported. So the marking appears once at the head and once at the foot of the document, which is what `main.ts` already intends, and the initiative name and domain go into the print-only banner text.

**A 768px-tall laptop.** 134 of chrome plus 43 of section header plus 46 of footer leaves 541px, under two question cards. The `@media (max-height: 800px)` rule drops the sticky section summary to static and compacts the stepper to 36px, recovering to 596px. If she reports it still feels cramped, the section summary is already the designated first cut.

**A phone.** Nobody fills a 176-question Protected B instrument on a phone, but it must not be broken. At `max-width: 1100px` the rail becomes a 32px `<details>` dropdown inside the sticky stepper block, same list, same markup. At `max-width: 700px` the five domain tabs become a horizontal scroll strip and the sticky section summary goes static. The existing `max-width: 620px` rules for `.ev-row` stay. The `.q-gutter` narrows from 2.9rem to 2rem and the id line under the number drops. Total sticky chrome on a phone: 86 plus 4 plus 40 plus 32, which is 162 of a 640px viewport. Tight but usable, and the question column is full width.

**Keyboard.** The known failure with sticky headers is that Tab moves focus to an element scrolled under the pinned stack. Mitigation is `scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--chrome-h) + var(--stepper-h) + 3.6rem)` on `.question`, `.card.section`, `textarea`, `.score-row` and `.evidence`; browsers honour `scroll-margin` for focus scrolling, not only for `scrollIntoView`. The roving tabindex is the other keyboard risk: implemented wrong it makes the score row unreachable. The rule is exactly one `tabindex="0"` in each `.score-row` at all times, on the checked button, or on the `0` button when nothing is checked, and the test asserts that count. ArrowRight, ArrowLeft, Home and End move focus and select, per the WAI-ARIA radiogroup pattern. When Not applicable is checked, all eleven buttons get `aria-disabled="true"` and `disabled`, and the row's tab stop moves to the checkbox.

**Screen reader.** Three specific risks. First, `<details>` open and closed state is announced inconsistently by older JAWS, so the rail item's `aria-current="true"` and the polite live region carry the state independently of the disclosure. Second, a live region that fires on every one of 176 score clicks becomes a stream of chatter, so the `.sr-live` write is debounced 600ms and says only the count, with a section-complete sentence when one lands. Third, `role="progressbar"` with only `aria-valuenow` reads as a bare percentage, so all three meters carry `aria-valuetext="34 of 176 questions answered"`, and the footer keeps its plain sentence as the text of record.

**Colour-only signalling.** The status edge is four pixels of Dan's ramp, which on its own would fail. It is never on its own: the gutter carries `✓`, the card carries `data-state`, the rail carries a glyph and a text count, the section summary carries `6 of 9 answered`, and the `.chosen` line spells the rung out in words. The dashed edge for Not applicable is a pattern, not a hue. In dark mode `--surface` on `--surface-2` is `#191c22` on `#21252d`, subtle, which is why every level of the nesting also carries a 1px border.

**Animation.** The one real hazard is replay. A full repaint would recreate every card and, if the class were derived from the model, replay 176 animations at once. It is not derived: `just-answered` exists only as a class added by a click handler on a node that the narrowed repaint no longer destroys, and it is removed on `animationend`. So a classification change, which still does a full repaint, replays nothing. The reduced-motion block turns off all four animations and both width transitions, and no state is conveyed by motion alone.

**The regression I would guard hardest.** Narrowing the repaint means several things that used to refresh for free now refresh only because `rollup()` writes them. If a reference is missed, a counter silently goes stale, and a stale progress meter on a survey instrument is worse than no meter. So `rollup()` should hold its targets in one typed record built once per paint, the test should assert the strip's `aria-valuenow`, the rail count, the section summary count and the footer sentence all agree after a score change, and the test should also assert they agree after a Not applicable toggle, which is the case where the denominator moves.

proposals.hierarchy:
Four levels, four different devices, no two levels sharing one.

**Domain — the sheet.** Nothing in the body. It is the frozen header inside `.chrome` (see navigation) plus the raised tab in the bottom bar. The domain never appears in scrollable flow, so it can never scroll away. The current `<h2>` that reads "Business Architecture 5.2" to a screen reader is fixed by moving the `.pill` out of the heading into a sibling with `aria-label="Domain score 5.2 out of 10"`.

**Section — the sheet's banded block.** `<section class="sheet-section" id="business--defining-the-current-state" data-sec="…">` — ids must be namespaced `${domain.id}--${section.id}` because `defining-the-current-state` is reused in all four domains. Deliberately **not** a `.card`: `.card` carries `page-break-inside: avoid`, which fights a 14-row block on print. It carries instead:

```css
.sheet-section { margin-bottom: 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--line);
  border-radius: var(--radius); overflow: hidden; background: var(--surface);
  box-shadow: var(--shadow); scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--chrome-h, 156px) + .5rem); }
.sheet-section-head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: .55rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
  padding: .4rem .6rem; background: var(--surface-2);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2); }
```

Contents in order: `<span class="sec-num">1</span>` (mono, matches the chip number), a real `<h3>` with the full label, `.pill.small`, `"5% of this domain"` (keep that exact string — `test/ui.mjs` asserts it), `"3 of 14 answered"`, the optional `.badge`, and a `Hide`/`Show` `button.ghost.small` with `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls`.

**The column-header strip** — the one device that makes it read as a sheet:

```html
<div class="colhead" aria-hidden="true">
  <span>#</span><span>Question</span><span>Score, 0 to 10</span><span>Notes &amp; evidence</span><span></span>
</div>
```
`.colhead { font-size: .68rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .06em; color: var(--ink-3); font-weight: 650; background: var(--surface); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line-2); }` sharing `.row-grid`'s exact template. It repeats **once per section, not once per page**, so a column label is never more than 14 rows away. `aria-hidden` because every row is self-describing to AT.

**Question — the row.** `<li class="question row" id="q-BU-Q12" data-qid="BU-Q12">` inside `<ul class="rows">`. Keeping `question` as a second class keeps four existing assertions alive. Three children: `.row-grid`, `.chosen`, `.drawer[hidden]`.

```css
.rows { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
.row { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
.row:first-child { border-top: 0; }
.row:nth-child(even) { background: var(--band); }
.row-grid { display: grid; align-items: center; gap: .55rem; padding: 0;
  grid-template-columns: 2.3rem minmax(0, 1fr) 20rem 5.5rem 1.6rem; }
.cell-num { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .72rem; color: var(--ink-3);
  text-align: right; padding: .42rem .4rem; align-self: stretch;
  display: grid; place-items: center end;
  background: var(--gutter); border-right: 1px solid var(--line); }
.cell-q, .cell-score, .cell-notes, .cell-state { padding: .42rem 0; }
.q-text { font-size: .93rem; font-weight: 550; margin: 0; }
```

Five separating devices stacked, where today there is one 1px hairline: the 1px row rule, the alternating `--band` fill, the full-bleed grey gutter with its own right rule, the vertical alignment of the score strip into a column, and the `--gutter`-tinted number itself. Two new tokens, both a lightness step and not a hue step, so they survive greyscale and every form of colour blindness:

```css
:root { --band: #f6f8fa; --gutter: #eef1f5; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { :root { --band: #1d2128; --gutter: #1a1e25; } }
```

`.cell-num` numbers run **continuously down the sheet, 1 to 61**, not restarting per section — that is Excel, and it gives position without reading. `sheetSection()` takes a running counter.

**Score — the strip.** Eleven contiguous segments in Dan's ramp, one grid, in its own column:

```css
.strip { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(11, 1fr); width: 100%; max-width: 19rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--line-2); border-radius: 7px; overflow: hidden; }
.seg { border: 0; border-left: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 0; box-shadow: none;
  min-height: 26px; padding: .26rem 0; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .78rem;
  font-weight: 600; background: var(--surface); color: var(--ink-3); }
.seg:first-child { border-left: 0; }
.seg[aria-checked="true"] { color: #fff; font-weight: 700; font-size: .84rem; }
.seg[aria-checked="true"].v7 { background: var(--s7); }        /* × 11 */
.seg.under.v7 { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--s7) 15%, var(--surface)); color: var(--ink-2); }
```
19rem ÷ 11 = 27.6px wide × 26px tall, clearing WCAG 2.2 target size. `.under` tints every segment below the chosen one, so the row reads as a filled data bar — the same idea as `.bar-fill` on the results page, and the reason a score is legible at a glance down a column of 61. Each segment keeps `score-btn` as a second class so three existing assertions survive.

The chosen score is **not** signalled by colour. It is signalled by the `.chosen` line directly underneath — `<b>7 — Scalable &amp; Secure.</b> The architecture is hardened…`, one line, `white-space: nowrap; overflow: hidden; text-overflow: ellipsis` — plus `aria-checked`, plus the weight and size bump, plus the fill boundary, which is a shape. Colour is decoration on top of four other cues.

**Drawer.** `<div class="drawer" hidden>` holding, unchanged: the per-question `details.ladder-box` **only when `q.anchors` exists**, the `q.help` paragraph, the `q.picklist` block, the justification `label.field`, and `evidenceEditor()` untouched. Always in the DOM (`hidden`, not a `display:none` class) so `querySelector` finds it, print can unhide it, and opening costs nothing. `.drawer { background: var(--surface); border-top: 1px solid var(--line-2); padding: .5rem .7rem .7rem 2.85rem; }` — explicitly `--surface` so a drawer never bands, which reads as the note popped out of the cell.

The rubric gives no question its own `anchors`, so **176 identical copies of the 11-rung ladder disappear** — about 2,250 nodes on the Business page. Replaced by one `<details class="card ladder-ref">` at the top of each sheet, and by a `.strip-hint` in `.cell-score` that shows a rung's name on `mouseover`/`focusin` of any segment and reverts to the chosen rung's on leave. Six lines, no repaint, teaches the scale in place. No code is deleted — the per-question path stays live behind `q.anchors`.

Measured against the survey's baseline, at 1180px: unanswered row 58px, answered 77px, section head plus column strip 62px. Business Architecture is **~4,014px unanswered and ~5,173px fully answered, against 20,190px today** — 22.4 viewports down to 4.5.

proposals.progress:
**Three readouts, all pinned, all fed by `scoring.ts` as it already stands — no new computation.**

1. **Overall**, in the bottom bar's `.footer-score`: a 9rem track plus `42/176` in mono, beside the existing `.pill`. `role="progressbar"`, `aria-valuemin="0"`, `aria-valuemax="176"`, `aria-valuenow="42"`, `aria-valuetext="42 of 176 questions answered"`, `aria-label="Overall progress"`. The muted sentence that is the only always-visible progress today stays as the accessible text.
2. **This sheet**, in the frozen bar: `12/61` plus a track, same ARIA against `DomainScore.answered` / `.total`.
3. **Each section and each sheet**: the fraction on every `.sec-chip` and every `.sheet-tab`, plus the reused 3px `.step-bar` on the tab.

She asked for a bar with or without a number. It gets both, because the number is the accessible name for the bar and because a fraction beats a percentage when a section has three questions.

```css
.prog { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: .4rem; }
.prog-track { width: 9rem; height: 6px; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--surface-2);
  box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--line); overflow: hidden; }
.prog-fill { display: block; height: 100%; border-radius: 999px; background: var(--accent);
  transition: width .25s ease; }
.prog-num { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: .74rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
.prog-num b { color: var(--ink); }
.prog.full .prog-fill { background: var(--good); }
```

**What makes a question answered: a score of 0 to 10, or the n/a box ticked. Nothing else.** Three reasons, and I am not hedging on this. `scoring.ts` already defines `answered` exactly that way and `total` as not-n/a, so the sheet tab, the frozen bar and the results page are arithmetically incapable of disagreeing. A justification requirement across 176 questions makes the tool unfinishable and manufactures filler, which is worse than silence for an assessor. And the pressure already exists in the right place: `views-review.ts` flags "No justification given" and "High score, nothing cited" to the assessor, and a person asking beats a form refusing.

**Justification is never mandatory. Evidence is never mandatory.** The single hard gate in this app stays the classification marking, because that is the only thing that cannot be fixed after the fact.

Encouragement gets a place instead of a gate. When a score is 8 or higher and the row has no evidence, the notes button's label changes from `Add` to `Cite it` and takes `.notes-btn.wanted` — a `--warn` dotted underline. Derived, no new state, non-blocking, and the *word changes*, so it is not a colour cue. It is the exact thing the reviewer will flag.

**Changing a score to a different number: nothing is lost and nothing resets.** The strip's `.under` fill moves, `.chosen` rewrites, `data-print` rewrites, the count is unchanged, the tick stays. **The completion animation does not replay** — it fires only on the transition from `null`/n/a to a number, tracked by reading the previous value inside the click handler before assignment. Justification and evidence belong to the question, not to the score, and are untouched. Clicking the already-chosen segment clears it (current behaviour, kept): the count decrements, the tick fades out over 120ms with no flourish, and re-scoring replays the animation honestly. No "are you sure", no warning that the note may now be stale — that is the overcomplication she asked me to avoid.

**The prerequisite: stop repainting the world on a score click.** Today `changed()` runs `paint()`, rebuilding 4,049 nodes and destroying node identity, so any animation started on a score click is thrown away mid-frame. Replace it with a `refreshTotals()` closure created in `renderSubmit`, holding references to the ~10 nodes that can change — the frozen bar's pill, `.prog-fill` width, `.prog-num`, each section head's pill and count, each `.sec-chip-frac`, each `.sheet-tab-frac` and `.step-bar i`, `.footer-score`, and the `.gate` strip — recomputing `score(rubric, a)` (trivial over 176 questions) and writing text and widths. `questionBlock` takes `refresh` for score and n/a; evidence **classification** changes keep calling the full `repaintApp()`, because the marking banner lives in the shell. This also ends the focus destruction and is the single most important engineering change in the proposal.

**The animations. Three tiers, one shot each, class removed on `animationend`.**

*The tick, 220ms.* `.cell-state` holds a 15×15 inline SVG check, `stroke="currentColor"`, `color: var(--good)`. On the null→scored transition it gets `.in`:
```css
.cell-state .tick { opacity: 0; }
.cell-state .tick.shown { opacity: 1; }
.cell-state .tick.in path { stroke-dasharray: 22; stroke-dashoffset: 22;
  animation: tick-draw .22s ease-out forwards; }
@keyframes tick-draw { to { stroke-dashoffset: 0; } }
```
The checkmark draws itself. `aria-hidden="true"` — the `.chosen` text is the state for AT, and it is already `aria-describedby` the radiogroup.

*The row wash, 600ms — this is the "sparkly".* `.row.just-done`:
```css
@keyframes row-done {
  0%   { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 22%, transparent); }
  100% { background: transparent; }
}
.row.just-done > .row-grid { animation: row-done .6s ease-out; }
```
A green wash across the row that fades to nothing. Not particles, not confetti: this file gets printed and put in front of a director general, and the animation has to look like it belongs to the same object.

*The section and sheet completion shine, 900ms.* When a section's last question lands, its `.sec-chip` and its section-head `.pill` get `.flash`; when a sheet's last question lands, the `.sheet-tab` does too, and turns `.done`:
```css
@keyframes shine { 0% { background-position: -140% 0; } 100% { background-position: 240% 0; } }
.sec-chip.flash, .sheet-tab.flash, .sheet-section-head .pill.flash {
  background-image: linear-gradient(100deg, transparent 35%,
    color-mix(in srgb, var(--good) 45%, transparent) 50%, transparent 65%);
  background-size: 220% 100%; background-repeat: no-repeat;
  animation: shine .9s ease-out 1;
}
```
A light sweep across the tab. Because the chip is pinned in the frozen bar, the reward is visible no matter how far down the sheet she is — which is the part the current build cannot do at all.

The quiet workhorse is the existing `transition: width .25s ease` on every bar fill. Every score click moves four bars 250ms.

**`prefers-reduced-motion`.** Extend the block that already exists for `marking-pulse`:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .marking-banner.unmarked { animation: none; }
  .row.just-done > .row-grid, .sec-chip.flash, .sheet-tab.flash,
  .sheet-section-head .pill.flash, .cell-state .tick.in path { animation: none; }
  .prog-fill, .step-bar i, .bar-fill { transition: none; }
  html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
}
```
Nothing is lost, only motion: the tick still appears (that is `.shown`, an opacity state, not the keyframe), the count still increments, the bars still jump to their new width, the tab still turns green. `matchMedia` is absent in jsdom, so never branch on it in TypeScript — keep the whole opt-out in CSS, where the file already keeps it.

proposals.risks:
**Print.** Rewritten, because today the score selector does not print, section titles do not print, 176 ladders expand, and the foot marking never appears.

```css
@media print {
  .topbar, .sitefoot, .actions, .filelabel, .sec-fold, .notes-btn,
  .sheet-tabs, .footer-actions, .frozen-sections, .ladder-ref, .colhead,
  .strip, .cell-state, .ladder-box { display: none !important; }
  .chrome, .sticky-footer, .frozen { position: static !important; box-shadow: none !important; }
  body { background: #fff; color: #000; font-size: 10pt; }
  .marking-banner { background: none !important; color: #000 !important; border-bottom: 2px solid #000; }
  .marking-banner.print-only { display: block !important; border-top: 2px solid #000; border-bottom: 0; }
  .sheet-section { break-inside: auto; border: 0; box-shadow: none; }
  .sheet-section-head { break-after: avoid; page-break-after: avoid; border-bottom: 1px solid #000;
    background: none; }
  .row { break-inside: avoid; page-break-inside: avoid; border-top: 1px solid #999; }
  .row:nth-child(even) { background: none; }
  .cell-num { background: none; }
  .row-grid { grid-template-columns: 2rem 1fr 9rem; }
  .cell-score::after { content: attr(data-print); font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  .drawer[hidden] { display: block !important; }
  .drawer:not(.has-content) { display: none !important; }
  .drawer textarea, .drawer .ev-row, .drawer .ev-row2 { display: none; }
  .just-print, .ev-print { display: block; }
  .evidence { background: none; border: 0; border-left: 2px solid #000; border-radius: 0; padding-left: .5rem; }
}
```
Two mechanisms carry it. `.cell-score` holds `data-print="7 / 10 — Scalable & Secure"`, written by `paintScores`, so `content: attr()` prints the answer with no extra node — this is the fix for the missing score. And form controls do not print their typed values reliably, so the justification is mirrored into a `<p class="just-print">` (and evidence title/location into `<p class="ev-print">`) on **`input`**, not on `change`, so a note typed and printed without leaving the field still prints. `.has-content` is set in the same closure that updates the notes button, so only drawers with something in them print. Removing 176 ladder copies is most of the page-count fix on its own; a full 176-question assessment lands near six to eight pages instead of unbounded.

**Phone, below 720px.** The grid cannot hold and I am not pretending it can.
```css
@media (max-width: 720px) {
  .body { max-width: none; }
  .row-grid { grid-template-columns: 2rem minmax(0,1fr) 3.4rem;
    grid-template-areas: "num q state" "num score score" "num notes notes"; row-gap: .3rem; }
  .cell-num { align-self: start; }
  .strip { max-width: none; } .seg { min-height: 30px; font-size: .74rem; }
  .colhead, .sheet-tab-frac, .sticky-footer .pill { display: none; }
  .frozen-sections { display: none; }        /* replaced by a native select */
  .sec-select { display: block; }
}
```
The row stacks into three bands inside a persistent number gutter; the strip goes full width (375 − 32 − 34 gutter ≈ 308px ÷ 11 = 28px segments, still over 24px); the sheet tabs stay and scroll sideways with fractions dropped. Two honest costs: the column-header strip is gone, so the row stops reading as a sheet and becomes a stacked card again — that is the metaphor breaking and it is the right call. And on a 667px phone, 92px of chrome + 64px frozen + 86px bottom leaves 425px, about three stacked rows. To claw back 30px the section chips collapse below 720px into a native `<select class="sec-select">` whose `onchange` sets `location.hash` — the phone convention, and about four lines. Nothing scrolls horizontally except the tab strips, which is deliberate and matches Excel.

**Keyboard.** The strip is one tab stop with roving `tabindex`: `0` on the checked segment or the first if none, `-1` on the rest. `keydown` on `.strip`: ArrowLeft/Up → v−1, ArrowRight/Down → v+1, Home → 0, End → 10, and arrow both moves and selects, per radiogroup convention. The existing shared `:focus-visible` rule already covers `button`, so segments get the 2px accent outline free — but `overflow: hidden` on `.strip` would clip an outline on the first and last segment, so `.seg:focus-visible { outline-offset: -3px; z-index: 1; position: relative; }`. Two real consequences: a screen-reader user sweeping the strip changes the score up to eleven times, firing eleven `localStorage` writes (harmless) and the completion animation on the first arrow (acceptable — it is the standard behaviour and departing from it to require Space-to-commit would be worse); and nobody can Tab directly onto a specific segment any more.

**Screen reader.** `role="radiogroup"` with `aria-labelledby` on the question's `<h4>` id and `aria-describedby` on the `.chosen` line, so the group is named by the question and described by the current rung. Row `id`s come from rubric data, so a `cssId()` sanitizer — `s.replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9_-]/g, '_')` — guards the whole anchoring scheme even though all 176 current ids are already safe. The tick SVG is `aria-hidden`. The remaining gap: no grid navigation and no column announcements, as argued above.

**Things that will actually bite, ranked.**
1. **jsdom 29.1.1 has no `IntersectionObserver` and no `matchMedia`.** `watchSections()` must be guarded with `typeof IntersectionObserver === 'function'` or `npm run test:ui` throws on load, and reduced-motion must stay entirely in CSS with no TypeScript branch.
2. **The sticky budget.** 156px top plus 62px bottom is 24% of a 900px laptop and 28% of a 768px one. If it feels tight the lever is the frozen bar's second row, and the fallback is the phone `<select>` at every width.
3. **`--chrome-h` staleness.** Measured once per paint; a window resize that rewraps `.topbar` without a paint leaves hash-jump offsets off by roughly 30px until the debounced `resize` handler runs. Mitigated, not eliminated.
4. **Banding versus drawers.** An open drawer inside an even row sits on `--surface` while its row is banded, so the stripe rhythm visibly breaks while a drawer is open. Intentional, and it will look like a bug to somebody once.
5. **`refreshTotals()` is a hand-maintained node list.** Add a field to the frozen bar or a fifth domain to the rubric and forget to wire it, and one readout silently goes stale — the failure mode the full repaint made impossible. Worth one assertion per readout in `test/ui.mjs`, and worth keeping the closure in a single named function rather than scattering it.

